@HostAddress = http://localhost:5125

### 0. Service info — lists the available endpoints
GET {{HostAddress}}/
Accept: application/json

### 1. List products (helper) — use this to find product ids for creating an invoice
#    Each product carries its brand and stock.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/products
Accept: application/json

### 1b. Brands — GENERATED extension, brand.Map<BrandDto>(mapper) creates a new DTO.
#     Note "isoCode" in the response: the entity spells it ISOCode and the DTO spells it
#     IsoCode, so this is case-insensitive matching working end to end, through the database.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/brands
Accept: application/json

### 1b2. One brand — the other overload, brand.Map(existingDto, mapper) copies onto an
#     object that already exists. "updatedInPlace" proves it is the same instance, and
#     "mapperDependency" proves AppMapper really is a DI service with a dependency.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/brands/1
Accept: application/json

### 1c. Stocks — same generated maps, but called DIRECTLY on the mapper instance
#     (mapper.Map<StockDto>(stock)) instead of through the extension method.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/stocks
Accept: application/json

### 1c2. One stock — the instance form of the update overload, mapper.Map(stock, existing)
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/stocks/1
Accept: application/json

### 1c3. Create a stock — the REVERSE map, StockDto -> Stock. Every request above goes
#     entity -> DTO; this one only compiles because AppMapper chains .ReverseMap().
#     The id below is ignored — the endpoint resets it so the database assigns the real one.
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/stocks
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Duhok Distribution Centre",
  "city": "Duhok",
  "code": "DHK-DC"
}

### 1c4. Read back what 1c3 created (id 5 on a fresh database) — both directions of the
#     round trip come from that one CreateMap line.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/stocks/5
Accept: application/json

### 1c5. Validation — a stock with no name returns 400 before any mapping happens
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/stocks
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "",
  "city": "Nowhere",
  "code": "NON-E"
}

### 2. List all invoices — full graph: invoice -> lines -> product -> brand + stock
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices
Accept: application/json

### 3. Get a single invoice by id — full graph
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices/1
Accept: application/json

### 4. Create an invoice
#    Only product id + quantity are sent; the server snapshots the current price.
#    The response is the created invoice with the full related graph.
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "customerName": "Layla Ibrahim",
  "customerEmail": "layla.ibrahim@example.com",
  "lines": [
    { "productId": 2, "quantity": 1 },
    { "productId": 10, "quantity": 2 },
    { "productId": 23, "quantity": 4 }
  ]
}

### 5. Get the invoice that was just created (id 4 on a fresh database)
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices/4
Accept: application/json

### 6. Not found — asking for an invoice that does not exist returns 404
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices/9999
Accept: application/json

### 7. Validation — empty lines returns 400
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "customerName": "No Lines",
  "customerEmail": "no.lines@example.com",
  "lines": []
}

### 8. Validation — unknown product id returns 400
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "customerName": "Bad Product",
  "customerEmail": "bad.product@example.com",
  "lines": [
    { "productId": 99999, "quantity": 1 }
  ]
}
